title: When the root moves
tags: [[Watcher]] Platform
kind: reference
summary: A repository whose root moved, vanished or was replaced is unloaded and listed as lost until it is loaded again where it is — never followed, never orphaned.


Every tracked path is relative to the root, so a root that is no longer there makes every
path read as gone. Acting on that orphans the whole repository: the watcher, still
holding watches or a filesystem mark, reports what happens at the new place under the old
root's names (an inotify source did exactly that — a file removed in the moved
directory was orphaned as if it had been removed under the old one).

''Detection.'' At the open, the daemon records the root directory's identity (`st_dev`,
`st_ino`). Every data request and every flush checks it with one `stat`: the path gone,
or another directory at it (moved away and one made in its place), is a lost root. The
check does not depend on the watch source, and covers a root renamed, removed, or on a
drive unplugged.

''Unloading.'' A lost root takes the repository out of service for good, even if the root
comes back:

* the daemon unloads it — tasks asked to stop, a rollback lock dropped, watcher and executor stopped, the store and its lock let go — on a thread of its own, since the executor can be the one that found the root gone and the unload joins it;
* until that is done, each flush drops what the watcher buffered, writing nothing;
* a warning in the repository's diagnostics says so once.

It stays known as ''lost'' until it is loaded again or unloaded: every request addressed to
it answers `503`, naming the old root and how to resume — reads included, unlike an
[[offline subtree|Offline subtrees]], since nothing about the files can be told any more —
and `GET /repos?lost=true` lists it with `lost: true`, which is what the GUI's
[[repos panel]] and `mf repo list` show. The plain `GET /repos` does not: it is the list
of repositories a client can use, and the GUI maps file paths to repositories with it —
a lost one there would claim a root that is no longer its own, or another directory now in
its place. Unloading a lost repository (`mf repo unload`, ''Forget'' in the panel) forgets
it.

''Resuming is explicit.'' The daemon does not follow the root, even a rename it could
prove (decided October 2026: a moved directory is the user's to confirm). Loading the
repository where it is now is an ordinary load, as [[Moving a repository]] describes:
`mf repo load NEW_ROOT`, or for an external `.metafolder/`, `root` corrected in its
`config.json` and `mf repo load --metafolder DIR`. Loading it by its old path works the
same once it is back there. A lost repository of the same uuid or name is no longer
listed once another is loaded.

What changed while it was lost is not replayed: the buffer was dropped. Nothing is
reconciled on its own after a load ([[Moving a repository]]); a removal made meanwhile
leaves a stale path, which `mf orphan detect` finds.

Left open ([[Watcher open questions]]): finding a repository that comes back on a
remounted drive by its volume identity, rather than waiting for a load.
